// A four-layer x32 SDR SDRAM memory card: two ISSI IS42S16400J dice, each
// sixteen bits wide, sharing one series-damped address and command bus and
// bringing a 32-bit data bus to a pair of dual-row carrier connectors, with
// the serial presence detect, thermal provision and supply filtering a real
// module carries, under the skew and impedance budgets a 143 MHz memory bus
// needs.
//
// Produced from a prompt through the Synth MCP server.
//
// WHAT THIS BOARD IS FOR. A length- and impedance-sensitive parallel
// interface. Everything here is subordinate to the bus: one rail in, no
// controller, no second clock domain. The subject is the eighty-odd nets
// that have to arrive together.
//
// WHY TWO DICE RATHER THAN ONE. A single x16 die gives sixteen data lines
// that each run point to point from the connector to one pad, which is the
// easy half of the problem. Widening to x32 is how memory is actually built,
// and it changes the question: address and command now fan out to two loads
// each, so every one of the nineteen shared lines is a branch whose two
// halves have to match, and the data bus splits into two groups of sixteen
// that are matched within a group rather than across all thirty-two.
//
// WHY THERE IS NO CONTROLLER ON THIS BOARD. A driver would need about
// thirty-six signal pins, honest SDRAM controller hardware, and a package
// whose pads a minimum track can leave. At JLCPCB's standard floor that last
// one wants 0.254 mm of gap between pads, and nothing in the registry has all
// three:
//
//   ice40hx4k_bg121   121 pins, a real controller, but a 0.8 mm BGA grid that
//                     escapes only through vias in its pads, and the FPGA
//                     video board already measured what that costs
//   rp2350            QFN-60 at 0.4 mm pitch: 0.200 mm of gap, and no SDRAM
//                     controller
//   stm32f103c8       LQFP-48 at 0.5 mm pitch: 0.200 mm of gap, and this die
//                     carries no FSMC
//   stm32g030         escapes, but seven pins
//   atmega328p        escapes, but no memory bus
//
// So the board is a memory card rather than a system: the bus terminates at
// two carrier connectors, which is where module length matching actually
// lives — a SO-DIMM matches from its dice to its edge fingers and no
// further. The TSOP-II's 0.8 mm two-row pads escape without a via at all, so
// no part of this board fails for want of room, and the skew measurement is
// about skew.
//
// WHY EVERY SHARED LINE IS SERIES DAMPED. Nineteen of the lines on the
// command connector reach two dice, and a 143 MHz edge arriving at a T sees
// two unterminated stubs and reflects off both. Twenty-two ohms in series at
// the source is what an unbuffered module does about it: it slows the edge
// into the branch and dissipates the reflection on the way back, without the
// static current a parallel termination to a reference rail would draw or
// the regulator that rail would need. Twenty-four resistors carry it — the
// nineteen shared lines, the four byte masks and the clock.
//
// It also changes what the matching problem is, which is the point of
// measuring it here. Each line is now two nets: a stub from the connector to
// its resistor, and a branch from the resistor to the dice. The stubs are
// nominally identical and the branches are where the two halves diverge, so
// the budgets split with them — `*_HOST` on the stub, the bus class on the
// branch. A total path budget is the sum of the two, which is the figure a
// controller datasheet quotes.
//
// THE DATA BUS IS NOT DAMPED, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. Every one of the
// thirty-two data lines runs from one connector pin to exactly one pad on one
// die. There is no branch to damp and no stub to reflect off, so a series
// resistor there would add a discontinuity and 32 parts to buy nothing. What
// the data bus needs is matching, which is what its two classes declare.
//
// WHY VDD AND VDDQ ARE SEPARATED BY A BEAD. The die brings its core supply
// out on three pins and its output-driver supply out on four, and it does
// that so the two can be filtered apart: thirty-two drivers switching into a
// 32-bit bus is the loudest thing on this card, and the core logic that has
// to sample a 143 MHz clock is the most sensitive. A ferrite bead between
// them gives the driver noise a high impedance back toward the core while
// costing a few tens of milliohms of DC, which keeps the two rails inside the
// 0.3 V of each other the datasheet asks for. Each rail then carries its own
// bulk capacitor and its own per-pin ceramics.
//
// VDDQ is a routed rail rather than a second poured plane. This stack has two
// inner layers: the first is the reference plane the F.Cu bus is measured
// against, and the second is left as a signal layer so the bus has somewhere
// to cross itself. A second pour would take that away for a rail that eight
// pads and one capacitor can reach on copper.
//
// SERIAL PRESENCE DETECT. A module that cannot say what it is has to be
// configured by hand, so the card carries the EEPROM a DIMM carries, on its
// own two-wire bus brought out on the command connector's last two pins with
// its own pull-ups. Its three address pins are strapped to ground, giving it
// the lowest address on that bus so a host finds it without probing, and its
// write-protect pin is strapped low so a host can program it. The bus is slow
// and it crosses the fast one, which is why it is declared as its own class
// with its own width and clearance rather than left to the default.
//
// THERMAL PROVISION. The dice are the only parts on this card that dissipate
// anything, and a host that cannot read the card's temperature cannot
// throttle it. A 10 k NTC in a divider against the rail brings that out as
// one analog line, which is what an SDR-era module did before the digital
// sensors of DDR3. It is deliberately not a digital sensor on the presence
// detect bus: the registry's TMP117 cannot have its address strapped, and a
// sensor whose address is decided by a floating pin is not a measurement.
//
// ESD. The presence detect lines and the clock arrive on connector pins a
// hand can touch while the card is out of its socket, and the EEPROM is the
// one part here that a static discharge can brick permanently. A dual rail
// clamp sits across those two lines at the connector.
//
// THE BUS, AND WHY IT IS FIVE CLASSES RATHER THAN ONE. A memory bus is not
// one group. The data lines have to track the clock, the address lines have
// to be stable before it, and the control lines are sampled on its edge.
// Declaring one tolerance across all of them would state a constraint nobody
// has:
//
//   SDRAM_DQL    16 data lines to U1, max_skew 2.5mm — the +/-100 mil the
//                SDRAM layout guides ask for within a data group
//   SDRAM_DQH    16 data lines to U2, max_skew 2.5mm — its own group, for
//                its own die, masked by its own two DQM lines
//   SDRAM_ADDR   12 address + 2 bank, max_skew 5mm, two loads each
//   SDRAM_CTRL   /RAS /CAS /WE /CS CKE and the four byte masks, max_skew 5mm
//   SDRAM_CLK    the clock alone, max_length 45mm
//
// with `SDRAM_ADDR_HOST`, `SDRAM_CTRL_HOST` and `SDRAM_CLK_HOST` carrying the
// stub side of the same lines on a tighter budget, because every stub on this
// card runs the same short distance from one connector to one resistor beside
// it.
//
// The two data groups are separate because nothing requires DQ0 and DQ31 to
// match: they are captured by different dice on different byte masks. What
// has to match is each group against the clock, and the clock is bounded by
// a length rather than a skew because the group check compares a class's
// members against each other and skips a class with fewer than two.
//
// All of them carry impedance 50ohm, because that is what an
// impedance-sensitive interface means: a budget on one line of eighty would
// not be one.
//
// THE CLOCK IS A T AFTER ITS RESISTOR. CLK arrives on one pin, meets its
// series resistor, and reaches two dice from there, so the card's job is to
// keep the two halves of the branch the same length. Putting the resistor at
// the connector rather than at the T is deliberate: it is a source
// termination, and a resistor in the middle of the branch would damp one die
// and split the other's path into a net of its own.
//
// IMPEDANCE comes from the stackup below, and the width comes from the
// stackup rather than from habit. Over the 0.2104 mm prepreg under F.Cu,
// Synth's estimator puts a 0.2 mm trace at 67.3 Ohm and a 0.35 mm trace at
// 50.0 Ohm, so 0.35 mm is what the bus classes declare. Only outer-layer
// microstrip is derived, so a line routed on B.Cu is referenced to the 3V3
// plane instead and W-SYNTH-IMPEDANCE-001 will say the figure is unverified
// there. That is accurate and stays stated.

board "sdram_memory" {
    layers 4
    manufacturer "jlcpcb"
    revision "C"

    // Four layers because a 143 MHz bus needs a continuous reference under
    // it. The 3V3 plane on In1.Cu is that reference and also feeds the
    // core supply pins without a star of traces across the board.
    stackup {
      copper 0.035mm
      insulator 0.2104mm er 4.4 material "FR4"
      copper 0.0152mm
      insulator 1.065mm er 4.6 material "FR4"
      copper 0.0152mm
      insulator 0.2104mm er 4.4 material "FR4"
      copper 0.035mm
    }

    // The 3V3 rail is the declared plane, and it takes the first inner layer,
    // so it is the copper the F.Cu bus is referenced to. Ground pours on the
    // outer layers, as it does on the other Class-C boards.
    //
    // Ground is deliberately *not* declared as a plane class. A declared
    // plane needs a stitching via beside every pad on it, and GND reaches
    // more than sixty endpoints including both TSOP-II packages' own pads: on
    // an 0.8 mm two-row package the exporter finds no legal position beside
    // those pads and refuses the export unless via-in-pad is allowed. The bus
    // escapes these packages with a plain track on the standard floor, and
    // buying a filled-via process to stitch a plane would give that up for a
    // return path the decoupling already provides — the sixteen capacitors
    // tie both rails to ground at every supply pin, which is what makes the
    // plane a valid AC reference.
    netclass "V3V3" {
      trace_width 0.6mm
      clearance 0.25mm
      plane
    }

    // The supply before the fuse, and the driver rail after the bead. Both
    // are wide copper rather than pours: one is four connector pins long and
    // the other reaches nine pads.
    netclass "RAIL_IN" {
      trace_width 0.8mm
      clearance 0.25mm
    }

    netclass "VDDQ" {
      trace_width 0.8mm
      clearance 0.25mm
    }

    netclass "GND" {
      trace_width 0.6mm
      clearance 0.25mm
    }

    // 0.35 mm over the 0.2104 mm prepreg is the 50 Ohm geometry for this
    // stackup — Synth's own estimator, not a rule of thumb — so every bus
    // class takes it. What differs between them is the spread each allows.
    netclass "SDRAM_DQL" {
      trace_width 0.35mm
      clearance 0.2mm
      impedance 50ohm
      max_skew 2.5mm
    }

    netclass "SDRAM_DQH" {
      trace_width 0.35mm
      clearance 0.2mm
      impedance 50ohm
      max_skew 2.5mm
    }

    netclass "SDRAM_ADDR" {
      trace_width 0.35mm
      clearance 0.2mm
      impedance 50ohm
      max_skew 5mm
    }

    netclass "SDRAM_CTRL" {
      trace_width 0.35mm
      clearance 0.2mm
      impedance 50ohm
      max_skew 5mm
    }

    // The stub side of every damped line: one connector pin to one resistor
    // sitting beside it. Nothing about these runs differs from line to line,
    // so they carry a tighter spread than the branches they feed, and the
    // total path budget is the two added together.
    netclass "SDRAM_ADDR_HOST" {
      trace_width 0.35mm
      clearance 0.2mm
      impedance 50ohm
      max_skew 3mm
    }

    netclass "SDRAM_CTRL_HOST" {
      trace_width 0.35mm
      clearance 0.2mm
      impedance 50ohm
      max_skew 3mm
    }

    // The clock is alone in its class on purpose: it is the reference every
    // other budget is measured against, and sharing a tolerance with the
    // signals it clocks would be circular. A length bounds each half of it
    // instead, which is the constraint a single net can carry.
    netclass "SDRAM_CLK_HOST" {
      trace_width 0.35mm
      clearance 0.25mm
      impedance 50ohm
      max_length 25mm
    }

    netclass "SDRAM_CLK" {
      trace_width 0.35mm
      clearance 0.25mm
      impedance 50ohm
      max_length 45mm
    }

    // Presence detect is two open-drain lines at 100 kHz crossing a bus at
    // 143 MHz. Narrow copper and a wider clearance is the right trade for
    // them: they have no impedance requirement of their own and the
    // clearance is what keeps them from coupling into what they cross.
    netclass "SPD" {
      trace_width 0.25mm
      clearance 0.3mm
    }

    // One analog line off a divider. Same reasoning, and it also wants to
    // stay away from the clock it runs beside.
    netclass "THERMAL" {
      trace_width 0.25mm
      clearance 0.3mm
    }

    // The rail arrives on the command connector, as a mezzanine's supply
    // does, through a resettable fuse: a card that can be inserted live can
    // also be inserted wrongly, and a polyfuse is what stops a short on this
    // board from being the host's problem. The bulk capacitor sits after it,
    // where it can supply the inrush the ceramics cannot.
    group "POWER ENTRY" color "#047857" {
      component F1: fuse "polyfuse" value "500mA"
      component C18: capacitor "c_generic_0805" value "10uF"
      component L1: inductor "ferrite_bead_0603" value "600R@100MHz"
      component C19: capacitor "c_generic_0805" value "22uF"
      component C17: capacitor "c_generic_0805" value "10uF"

      // 0.1 ohm in each rail, so the monitor has something to measure across.
      // At the card's ~200 mA that is 20 mV, well inside the monitor's 163.8
      // mV full scale and 4 mW in a 2010 part rated for half a watt. R31 sees
      // the whole module, R32 only the driver rail, so the core current is
      // the difference rather than a third shunt.
      //
      // R31 sits ahead of the fuse rather than behind it, which is not the
      // conventional place for a shunt. The reason is the plane: the monitor
      // has to tap both ends of whatever it measures, its pads are on an
      // 0.65 mm grid, and a declared plane needs a stitching via beside every
      // pad on it — which there is no room for beside a pad that fine. Ahead
      // of the fuse both ends of the shunt are routed copper, and what channel
      // one then reports is the current the host actually delivers, fuse drop
      // included.
      component R31: resistor "r_generic_2010" value "0.1R"
      component R32: resistor "r_generic_2010" value "0.1R"
      // Six pads, so bring-up and a production test need no wire soldered to
      // a component: both rails, ground, the thermal line and the two
      // presence detect lines. The bus is deliberately not probed — a stub
      // off a 143 MHz line to a test pad is a stub.
      component TP1: testpoint "testpoint_pad_1_5" {
        placement_hint { region: top_edge edge: top priority: soft }
      }
      component TP2: testpoint "testpoint_pad_1_5" {
        placement_hint { region: top_edge edge: top priority: soft }
      }
      component TP3: testpoint "testpoint_pad_1_5" {
        placement_hint { region: top_edge edge: top priority: soft }
      }
      component TP4: testpoint "testpoint_pad_1_5" {
        placement_hint { region: top_edge edge: top priority: soft }
      }
      component TP5: testpoint "testpoint_pad_1_5" {
        placement_hint { region: top_edge edge: top priority: soft }
      }
      component TP6: testpoint "testpoint_pad_1_5" {
        placement_hint { region: top_edge edge: top priority: soft }
      }

      connect R31.p2 -> F1.p1  as "RAIL_FUSED" class "RAIL_IN"
      connect F1.p2  -> C18.p1 as "V3V3" class "V3V3"
      connect F1.p2  -> C17.p1 as "V3V3"
      connect F1.p2  -> L1.p1  as "V3V3"
      connect L1.p2  -> R32.p1 as "VDDQ_SENSE" class "VDDQ"
      connect R32.p2 -> C19.p1 as "VDDQ" class "VDDQ"
      connect C18.p2 -> C19.p2 as "GND"  class "GND"
      connect C17.p2 -> C19.p2 as "GND"

      // Both rails are brought out where a probe can reach them, because the
      // one measurement this card cannot infer is what its supply does under
      // a write burst.
      connect TP1.p1 -> C17.p1 as "V3V3"
      connect TP2.p1 -> C19.p1 as "VDDQ"
      connect TP3.p1 -> C17.p2 as "GND"
    }

    // U1 takes the low half of the data bus. The dice sit beside each other in the middle of the card
    // with the connectors on either side, so the shared lines reach both over
    // comparable copper — which is what makes their branch budget achievable
    // rather than merely declared.
    //
    // One 100 nF per supply pin in 0402, because the seven pins are on four
    // sides of an 0.8 mm package and a shared capacitor is only local to one
    // of them: C1, C2, C3 on the core rail, C4, C5, C6, C7 on the
    // driver rail, and C8 as the bulk reservoir for both.
    group "SDRAM LOW HALF" color "#1d4ed8" {
      component U1: memory "is42s16400j_sdram" {
        placement_hint { region: centre priority: hard }
      }

      component C1: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF" { placement_hint { near: "U1" priority: hard } }
      component C2: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF" { placement_hint { near: "U1" priority: hard } }
      component C3: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF" { placement_hint { near: "U1" priority: hard } }
      component C4: capacitor "c_generic_0402" value "100nF" { placement_hint { near: "U1" priority: hard } }
      component C5: capacitor "c_generic_0402" value "100nF" { placement_hint { near: "U1" priority: hard } }
      component C6: capacitor "c_generic_0402" value "100nF" { placement_hint { near: "U1" priority: hard } }
      component C7: capacitor "c_generic_0402" value "100nF" { placement_hint { near: "U1" priority: hard } }
      component C8: capacitor "c_generic_0805" value "22uF"  { placement_hint { near: "U1" priority: soft } }

      // Every rail endpoint names its net. A `connect` with no `as` makes a
      // net of its own, so leaving the later ones unnamed strands each supply
      // pin on a two-endpoint net with nothing but its own capacitor.
      connect U1.vdd_0  -> C1.p1 as "V3V3"
      connect U1.vdd_1  -> C2.p1 as "V3V3"
      connect U1.vdd_2  -> C3.p1 as "V3V3"
      connect U1.vddq_0 -> C4.p1 as "VDDQ"
      connect U1.vddq_1 -> C5.p1 as "VDDQ"
      connect U1.vddq_2 -> C6.p1 as "VDDQ"
      connect U1.vddq_3 -> C7.p1 as "VDDQ"
      connect U1.vdd_0  -> C8.p1 as "V3V3"

      connect C1.p2 -> U1.gnd_0  as "GND"
      connect C2.p2 -> U1.gnd_1  as "GND"
      connect C3.p2 -> U1.gnd_2  as "GND"
      connect C4.p2 -> U1.gndq_0 as "GND"
      connect C5.p2 -> U1.gndq_1 as "GND"
      connect C6.p2 -> U1.gndq_2 as "GND"
      connect C7.p2 -> U1.gndq_3 as "GND"
      connect C8.p2 -> U1.gnd_0  as "GND"

      // The two no-connect balls are left unconnected, which is what the
      // datasheet asks for; tying them to anything would be inventing a net.
    }

    // U2 takes the high half. The dice sit beside each other in the middle of the card
    // with the connectors on either side, so the shared lines reach both over
    // comparable copper — which is what makes their branch budget achievable
    // rather than merely declared.
    //
    // One 100 nF per supply pin in 0402, because the seven pins are on four
    // sides of an 0.8 mm package and a shared capacitor is only local to one
    // of them: C9, C10, C11 on the core rail, C12, C13, C14, C15 on the
    // driver rail, and C16 as the bulk reservoir for both.
    group "SDRAM HIGH HALF" color "#1e40af" {
      component U2: memory "is42s16400j_sdram" {
        placement_hint { region: centre priority: hard }
      }

      component C9: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF" { placement_hint { near: "U2" priority: hard } }
      component C10: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF" { placement_hint { near: "U2" priority: hard } }
      component C11: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF" { placement_hint { near: "U2" priority: hard } }
      component C12: capacitor "c_generic_0402" value "100nF" { placement_hint { near: "U2" priority: hard } }
      component C13: capacitor "c_generic_0402" value "100nF" { placement_hint { near: "U2" priority: hard } }
      component C14: capacitor "c_generic_0402" value "100nF" { placement_hint { near: "U2" priority: hard } }
      component C15: capacitor "c_generic_0402" value "100nF" { placement_hint { near: "U2" priority: hard } }
      component C16: capacitor "c_generic_0805" value "22uF"  { placement_hint { near: "U2" priority: soft } }

      // Every rail endpoint names its net. A `connect` with no `as` makes a
      // net of its own, so leaving the later ones unnamed strands each supply
      // pin on a two-endpoint net with nothing but its own capacitor.
      connect U2.vdd_0  -> C9.p1 as "V3V3"
      connect U2.vdd_1  -> C10.p1 as "V3V3"
      connect U2.vdd_2  -> C11.p1 as "V3V3"
      connect U2.vddq_0 -> C12.p1 as "VDDQ"
      connect U2.vddq_1 -> C13.p1 as "VDDQ"
      connect U2.vddq_2 -> C14.p1 as "VDDQ"
      connect U2.vddq_3 -> C15.p1 as "VDDQ"
      connect U2.vdd_0  -> C16.p1 as "V3V3"

      connect C9.p2 -> U2.gnd_0  as "GND"
      connect C10.p2 -> U2.gnd_1  as "GND"
      connect C11.p2 -> U2.gnd_2  as "GND"
      connect C12.p2 -> U2.gndq_0 as "GND"
      connect C13.p2 -> U2.gndq_1 as "GND"
      connect C14.p2 -> U2.gndq_2 as "GND"
      connect C15.p2 -> U2.gndq_3 as "GND"
      connect C16.p2 -> U2.gnd_0  as "GND"
    }

    // Two passives that decide what the dice do before the controller says
    // anything, and they sit on the branch side of the damping so the dice
    // see them even with the host's drivers in high impedance. CKE low holds
    // both dice out of any command the bus happens to be carrying while the
    // host is in reset, and /CS high keeps them deselected; a floating input
    // on either pin leaves that to chance.
    group "BUS IDLE" color "#a16207" {
      component R1: resistor "r_generic_0603" value "10k"
      component R2: resistor "r_generic_0603" value "10k"

      connect R1.p1 -> U1.cke as "CKE"
      connect R1.p2 -> U1.gnd_0 as "GND"
      connect R2.p1 -> U1.cs as "CS_N"
      connect R2.p2 -> U1.vdd_0 as "V3V3"
    }

    // The EEPROM a module carries so a host does not have to be told what is
    // on the card. Address strapped to zero, write-protect strapped low, one
    // ceramic of its own, and the clamp across the two lines that leave the
    // board.
    //
    // WHAT THE DEVICE HOLDS. Not arbitrary bytes: a fixed 32-byte record at
    // offset 0, little-endian, so a host can read a known structure rather
    // than a convention nobody wrote down.
    //
    //   0x00  u8    format version, 0x01
    //   0x01  u8    record length, 32
    //   0x02  u16   organisation: 0x2002 = two x16 devices, one rank
    //   0x04  u32   capacity in mebibytes, 16
    //   0x08  u16   maximum clock in MHz, 143
    //   0x0A  u8    CAS latency, 3
    //   0x0B  u8    supply, tenths of a volt, 33
    //   0x0C  char  board identifier, 8 bytes, "SDRAMX32"
    //   0x14  u8    board revision, ASCII, 'C'
    //   0x15  u8    BOM revision, 1
    //   0x16  u8    thermal sensor: 0x01 = NTC on the analog line
    //   0x17  u8    power monitor: 0x01 = three-channel, I2C 0x40
    //   0x18  u32   reserved, zero
    //   0x1C  u16   reserved, zero
    //   0x1E  u16   CRC-16/CCITT-FALSE over bytes 0x00..0x1D
    //
    // This is the card's own schema and it is deliberately not called SPD: a
    // JEDEC presence-detect image has a layout and a checksum of its own, and
    // claiming compatibility without meeting it would be worse than having no
    // schema at all. Nothing in this repository programs the device, so a
    // card built from these files reads back whatever the part shipped with —
    // the schema is what a programming step would write, not what is there.
    group "PRESENCE DETECT" color "#7c3aed" {
      component U3: memory "24lc256_eeprom"
      component C20: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF" {
        placement_hint { near: "U3" priority: hard }
      }
      component R3: resistor "r_generic_0603" value "4.7k"
      component R4: resistor "r_generic_0603" value "4.7k"
      component D1: diode "prtr5v0u2x" {
        placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: soft }
      }

      connect U3.vdd -> C20.p1 as "V3V3"
      connect C20.p2 -> U3.gnd as "GND"

      // Address zero and writable: three straps and one more, all to ground.
      // An unstrapped address pin on an I2C device is an address nobody can
      // predict, and an unstrapped write-protect is a module that cannot be
      // programmed.
      connect U3.a0 -> U3.gnd as "GND"
      connect U3.a1 -> U3.gnd as "GND"
      connect U3.a2 -> U3.gnd as "GND"
      connect U3.wp -> U3.gnd as "GND"

      connect U3.sda -> R3.p1 as "SPD_SDA" class "SPD"
      connect U3.scl -> R4.p1 as "SPD_SCL" class "SPD"
      connect R3.p2 -> U3.vdd as "V3V3"
      connect R4.p2 -> U3.vdd as "V3V3"

      connect TP5.p1 -> U3.sda as "SPD_SDA"
      connect TP6.p1 -> U3.scl as "SPD_SCL"

      connect D1.io1 -> U3.sda as "SPD_SDA"
      connect D1.io2 -> U3.scl as "SPD_SCL"
      connect D1.vcc -> U3.vdd as "V3V3"
      connect D1.gnd -> U3.gnd as "GND"
    }

    // What the module actually draws, rather than what it was estimated to
    // draw. One device on the presence detect bus measures both rails and
    // costs no connector pins, which is the only reason it can be here: both
    // connectors are full.
    //
    // Channel 1 is across R31 and sees the whole card, fuse included; two is
    // across R32 and sees the driver rail, so core current is the difference.
    // Channel 3 has both inputs on V3V3: no current, and the bus voltage for
    // free. WARNING, CRITICAL, PV and TC are open-drain outputs with nowhere
    // to go on this card and are left unconnected.
    group "POWER MONITOR" color "#0e7490" {
      component U4: sensor "ina3221_power_monitor"
      component C21: capacitor "c_generic_0603" value "100nF" {
        placement_hint { near: "U4" priority: hard }
      }

      // The monitor runs off the fused rail rather than the plane, so none of
      // its 0.65 mm pads sits on a net that needs a stitching via beside it.
      connect U4.vs  -> C21.p1 as "RAIL_FUSED"
      connect C21.p2 -> U4.gnd as "GND"
      connect U4.vpu -> U4.vs  as "RAIL_FUSED"

      // Address 0x40, and the exposed pad on ground: TI's land pattern asks
      // for it to be soldered, and ground is the only plane it can reach.
      connect U4.a0 -> U4.gnd as "GND"
      connect U4.ep -> U4.gnd as "GND"

      connect U4.sda -> U3.sda as "SPD_SDA"
      connect U4.scl -> U3.scl as "SPD_SCL"

      connect U4.in1_p -> R31.p1 as "RAIL_IN"
      connect U4.in1_n -> R31.p2 as "RAIL_FUSED"
      connect U4.in2_p -> R32.p1 as "VDDQ_SENSE"
      connect U4.in2_n -> R32.p2 as "VDDQ"
      connect U4.in3_p -> R31.p2 as "RAIL_FUSED"
      connect U4.in3_n -> R31.p2 as "RAIL_FUSED"
    }

    // One analog line that says how hot the dice are. The NTC sits between
    // the two packages, where the copper it is soldered to is the copper they
    // heat; a divider against the rail turns it into a voltage the host can
    // read without a current source of its own.
    group "THERMAL" color "#ea580c" {
      component R5: resistor "r_thermistor_ntc_10k" value "10k" {
        placement_hint { near: "U2" priority: soft }
      }
      component R6: resistor "r_generic_0603" value "10k" {
        placement_hint { near: "R5" priority: soft }
      }

      connect R6.p1 -> U1.vdd_0 as "V3V3"
      connect R6.p2 -> R5.p1 as "THERM" class "THERMAL"
      connect R5.p2 -> U1.gnd_0 as "GND"
      connect TP4.p1 -> R5.p1 as "THERM"
    }

    // J1 carries the thirty-two data lines with a ground every four. Eight
    // grounds in forty pins is what the connector leaves room for once the
    // bus has its pins; the plane does the rest of the return. The header is
    // odd-even numbered, so consecutive pins sit across the two rows and
    // every group of four data lines has a ground on one row beside it.
    //
    // Nothing here is damped: each of these lines runs from one pin to one
    // pad on one die.
    group "DATA BUS" color "#b91c1c" {
      component J1: connector "header_2x20" {
        placement_hint { region: right_edge edge: right priority: hard }
      }

      connect J1.p1  -> U1.gnd_0   as "GND" class "GND"
      connect J1.p2  -> U1.dq0     as "DQ0" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p3  -> U1.dq1     as "DQ1" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p4  -> U1.dq2     as "DQ2" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p5  -> U1.dq3     as "DQ3" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p6  -> U1.gndq_0  as "GND"
      connect J1.p7  -> U1.dq4     as "DQ4" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p8  -> U1.dq5     as "DQ5" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p9  -> U1.dq6     as "DQ6" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p10 -> U1.dq7     as "DQ7" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p11 -> U1.gndq_1  as "GND"
      connect J1.p12 -> U1.dq8     as "DQ8" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p13 -> U1.dq9     as "DQ9" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p14 -> U1.dq10    as "DQ10" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p15 -> U1.dq11    as "DQ11" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p16 -> U1.gndq_2  as "GND"
      connect J1.p17 -> U1.dq12    as "DQ12" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p18 -> U1.dq13    as "DQ13" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p19 -> U1.dq14    as "DQ14" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p20 -> U1.dq15    as "DQ15" class "SDRAM_DQL"
      connect J1.p21 -> U2.gnd_0   as "GND"
      connect J1.p22 -> U2.dq0     as "DQ16" class "SDRAM_DQH"
      connect J1.p23 -> U2.dq1     as "DQ17" class "SDRAM_DQH"
      connect J1.p24 -> U2.dq2     as "DQ18" class "SDRAM_DQH"
      connect J1.p25 -> U2.dq3     as "DQ19" class "SDRAM_DQH"
      connect J1.p26 -> U2.gndq_0  as "GND"
      connect J1.p27 -> U2.dq4     as "DQ20" class "SDRAM_DQH"
      connect J1.p28 -> U2.dq5     as "DQ21" class "SDRAM_DQH"
      connect J1.p29 -> U2.dq6     as "DQ22" class "SDRAM_DQH"
      connect J1.p30 -> U2.dq7     as "DQ23" class "SDRAM_DQH"
      connect J1.p31 -> U2.gndq_1  as "GND"
      connect J1.p32 -> U2.dq8     as "DQ24" class "SDRAM_DQH"
      connect J1.p33 -> U2.dq9     as "DQ25" class "SDRAM_DQH"
      connect J1.p34 -> U2.dq10    as "DQ26" class "SDRAM_DQH"
      connect J1.p35 -> U2.dq11    as "DQ27" class "SDRAM_DQH"
      connect J1.p36 -> U2.gndq_2  as "GND"
      connect J1.p37 -> U2.dq12    as "DQ28" class "SDRAM_DQH"
      connect J1.p38 -> U2.dq13    as "DQ29" class "SDRAM_DQH"
      connect J1.p39 -> U2.dq14    as "DQ30" class "SDRAM_DQH"
      connect J1.p40 -> U2.dq15    as "DQ31" class "SDRAM_DQH"
    }

    // J2 carries the address, bank, command and mask lines, the clock, the
    // supply and the sideband. Every line on this connector but the four
    // masks reaches both dice, so each is a two-load branch and the class
    // budget is what holds the two halves together — and each one reaches
    // them through its own series resistor, so the stub and the branch are
    // separate nets with separate budgets.
    //
    // The clock has a ground on both sides, which is the one place on this
    // connector where the pinout is chosen for the signal rather than for
    // the pin count. The last two pins carry presence detect and p30 the
    // thermal line: a module puts its sideband at one end of its edge, away
    // from the bus.
    group "COMMAND BUS" color "#be185d" {
      component J2: connector "header_2x20" {
        placement_hint { region: left_edge edge: left priority: hard }
      }

      // Twenty-four 0402 resistors, each beside the connector pin it damps.
      component R7: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R8: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R9: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R10: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R11: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R12: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R13: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R14: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R15: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R16: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R17: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R18: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R19: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R20: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R21: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R22: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R23: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R24: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R25: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R26: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R27: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R28: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R29: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }
      component R30: resistor "r_generic_0402" value "22R" { placement_hint { near: "J2" priority: hard } }

      connect J2.p1  -> U1.gnd_0   as "GND" class "GND"
      connect J2.p2  -> R7.p1     as "CLK_H" class "SDRAM_CLK_HOST"
      connect R7.p2 -> U1.clk     as "CLK" class "SDRAM_CLK"
      connect R7.p2 -> U2.clk     as "CLK"
      connect J2.p3  -> U1.gnd_1   as "GND"
      connect J2.p4  -> R8.p1     as "CKE_H" class "SDRAM_CTRL_HOST"
      connect R8.p2 -> U1.cke     as "CKE" class "SDRAM_CTRL"
      connect R8.p2 -> U2.cke     as "CKE"
      connect J2.p5  -> R9.p1     as "CS_N_H" class "SDRAM_CTRL_HOST"
      connect R9.p2 -> U1.cs      as "CS_N" class "SDRAM_CTRL"
      connect R9.p2 -> U2.cs      as "CS_N"
      connect J2.p6  -> U1.gnd_2   as "GND"
      connect J2.p7  -> R10.p1     as "RAS_N_H" class "SDRAM_CTRL_HOST"
      connect R10.p2 -> U1.ras     as "RAS_N" class "SDRAM_CTRL"
      connect R10.p2 -> U2.ras     as "RAS_N"
      connect J2.p8  -> R11.p1     as "CAS_N_H" class "SDRAM_CTRL_HOST"
      connect R11.p2 -> U1.cas     as "CAS_N" class "SDRAM_CTRL"
      connect R11.p2 -> U2.cas     as "CAS_N"
      connect J2.p9  -> R12.p1     as "WE_N_H" class "SDRAM_CTRL_HOST"
      connect R12.p2 -> U1.we      as "WE_N" class "SDRAM_CTRL"
      connect R12.p2 -> U2.we      as "WE_N"
      connect J2.p10 -> U1.gndq_0  as "GND"
      connect J2.p11 -> R13.p1     as "DQM0_H" class "SDRAM_CTRL_HOST"
      connect R13.p2 -> U1.ldqm    as "DQM0" class "SDRAM_CTRL"
      connect J2.p12 -> R14.p1     as "DQM1_H" class "SDRAM_CTRL_HOST"
      connect R14.p2 -> U1.udqm    as "DQM1" class "SDRAM_CTRL"
      connect J2.p13 -> R15.p1     as "DQM2_H" class "SDRAM_CTRL_HOST"
      connect R15.p2 -> U2.ldqm    as "DQM2" class "SDRAM_CTRL"
      connect J2.p14 -> R16.p1     as "DQM3_H" class "SDRAM_CTRL_HOST"
      connect R16.p2 -> U2.udqm    as "DQM3" class "SDRAM_CTRL"
      connect J2.p15 -> U1.gndq_1  as "GND"
      connect J2.p16 -> R17.p1     as "A0_H" class "SDRAM_ADDR_HOST"
      connect R17.p2 -> U1.a0      as "A0" class "SDRAM_ADDR"
      connect R17.p2 -> U2.a0      as "A0"
      connect J2.p17 -> R18.p1     as "A1_H" class "SDRAM_ADDR_HOST"
      connect R18.p2 -> U1.a1      as "A1" class "SDRAM_ADDR"
      connect R18.p2 -> U2.a1      as "A1"
      connect J2.p18 -> R19.p1     as "A2_H" class "SDRAM_ADDR_HOST"
      connect R19.p2 -> U1.a2      as "A2" class "SDRAM_ADDR"
      connect R19.p2 -> U2.a2      as "A2"
      connect J2.p19 -> R20.p1     as "A3_H" class "SDRAM_ADDR_HOST"
      connect R20.p2 -> U1.a3      as "A3" class "SDRAM_ADDR"
      connect R20.p2 -> U2.a3      as "A3"
      connect J2.p20 -> U1.gndq_2  as "GND"
      connect J2.p21 -> R21.p1     as "A4_H" class "SDRAM_ADDR_HOST"
      connect R21.p2 -> U1.a4      as "A4" class "SDRAM_ADDR"
      connect R21.p2 -> U2.a4      as "A4"
      connect J2.p22 -> R22.p1     as "A5_H" class "SDRAM_ADDR_HOST"
      connect R22.p2 -> U1.a5      as "A5" class "SDRAM_ADDR"
      connect R22.p2 -> U2.a5      as "A5"
      connect J2.p23 -> R23.p1     as "A6_H" class "SDRAM_ADDR_HOST"
      connect R23.p2 -> U1.a6      as "A6" class "SDRAM_ADDR"
      connect R23.p2 -> U2.a6      as "A6"
      connect J2.p24 -> R24.p1     as "A7_H" class "SDRAM_ADDR_HOST"
      connect R24.p2 -> U1.a7      as "A7" class "SDRAM_ADDR"
      connect R24.p2 -> U2.a7      as "A7"
      connect J2.p25 -> U1.gndq_3  as "GND"
      connect J2.p26 -> R25.p1     as "A8_H" class "SDRAM_ADDR_HOST"
      connect R25.p2 -> U1.a8      as "A8" class "SDRAM_ADDR"
      connect R25.p2 -> U2.a8      as "A8"
      connect J2.p27 -> R26.p1     as "A9_H" class "SDRAM_ADDR_HOST"
      connect R26.p2 -> U1.a9      as "A9" class "SDRAM_ADDR"
      connect R26.p2 -> U2.a9      as "A9"
      connect J2.p28 -> R27.p1     as "A10_H" class "SDRAM_ADDR_HOST"
      connect R27.p2 -> U1.a10     as "A10" class "SDRAM_ADDR"
      connect R27.p2 -> U2.a10     as "A10"
      connect J2.p29 -> R28.p1     as "A11_H" class "SDRAM_ADDR_HOST"
      connect R28.p2 -> U1.a11     as "A11" class "SDRAM_ADDR"
      connect R28.p2 -> U2.a11     as "A11"
      connect J2.p30 -> R5.p1     as "THERM"
      connect J2.p31 -> R29.p1     as "BA0_H" class "SDRAM_ADDR_HOST"
      connect R29.p2 -> U1.ba0     as "BA0" class "SDRAM_ADDR"
      connect R29.p2 -> U2.ba0     as "BA0"
      connect J2.p32 -> R30.p1     as "BA1_H" class "SDRAM_ADDR_HOST"
      connect R30.p2 -> U1.ba1     as "BA1" class "SDRAM_ADDR"
      connect R30.p2 -> U2.ba1     as "BA1"
      connect J2.p33 -> U2.gnd_0   as "GND"
      connect J2.p34 -> R31.p1    as "RAIL_IN" class "RAIL_IN"
      connect J2.p35 -> R31.p1    as "RAIL_IN"
      connect J2.p36 -> U2.gndq_3  as "GND"
      connect J2.p37 -> R31.p1    as "RAIL_IN"
      connect J2.p38 -> R31.p1    as "RAIL_IN"

      // The supply arrives on four pins and meets the fuse before anything
      // else on the card, so the whole board is behind it. Presence detect
      // leaves on the last two pins, at the opposite end of the connector
      // from the clock.
      connect J2.p39 -> U3.sda as "SPD_SDA"
      connect J2.p40 -> U3.scl as "SPD_SCL"
    }

    group "MECHANICAL" color "#525252" {
      component H1: mounting_hole "mounting_hole_m2_5" { placement_hint { region: top_left priority: hard } }
      component H2: mounting_hole "mounting_hole_m2_5" { placement_hint { region: top_right priority: hard } }
      component H3: mounting_hole "mounting_hole_m2_5" { placement_hint { region: bottom_left priority: hard } }
      component H4: mounting_hole "mounting_hole_m2_5" { placement_hint { region: bottom_right priority: hard } }
      // The four holes take the corners. The fiducials are deliberately
      // unhinted: a fiducial pinned to a corner a hole already holds lands
      // on top of it, and KiCad reports the pair as overlapping courtyards.
      component FID1: fiducial "fiducial_1mm"
      component FID2: fiducial "fiducial_1mm"
    }
}
